Subject: sw_engine: shapes with a blend method draw into 8-bit (matte, mask) surfaces

A shape with a blend method other than Normal is drawn through the blending rasterizers, which
support 32-bit surfaces only and give up on the 8-bit ones that mattes and masks are drawn
into. Such a shape left no coverage at all, so a matte that holds one (a soft-light stroke on
the matte's shape, say) hid everything under it (Lordicon: the content lines of a newspaper
icon disappeared).

An 8-bit surface holds coverage only, which a blend method does not change: there the shape is
drawn as a normal one.

Origin: Lordicon.
Base: thorvg/thorvg main 72a66415 (1.2.0). License: MIT, like ThorVG.
---
--- a/src/renderer/cpu_engine/tvgSwRaster.cpp
+++ b/src/renderer/cpu_engine/tvgSwRaster.cpp
@@ -68,9 +68,11 @@
     return (a << 24 | r << 16 | g << 8 | b);
 }
 
+//8-bit surfaces (mattes, masks) hold coverage only: a blend method changes colours, and the
+//coverage of a blended shape is that of a normal one.
 static inline bool _blending(const SwSurface* surface)
 {
-    return (surface->blender) ? true : false;
+    return surface->blender && surface->channelSize == sizeof(uint32_t);
 }
 
